{
  "title": "Elbow Arthroscopy: Indications & Approach",
  "url": "https://www.orthobullets.com/shoulder-and-elbow/3088/elbow-arthroscopy-indications-and-approach",
  "subtitle": "Updated: Mar 1 2025",
  "sections": [
    {
      "text": "Introduction",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Indications",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "loose body removal",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "4",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "osteophyte debridement",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "synovectomy",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "capsular releases for stiffness",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "osteochondritis dissecans of capitellum",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "lateral epicondylitis",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "debridement for septic arthritis",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Contraindications",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "prior trauma",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "surgical scarring",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "**previous ulnar nerve transposition**",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "4",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "**ulnar nerve subluxation is not an absolute contraindication**, but it should be identified prior to surgery, especially with prior submuscular or intramuscular transposition",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "5",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Advantages",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "improved articular visualization",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "decreased postoperative pain",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "faster postoperative recovery",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Disadvantages",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "technically demanding",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "**high risk of damage to neurovascular structures** due to proximity to the joint",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "4",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Positioning",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Patient position may be",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "supine",
              "table": null,
              "images": [
                {
                  "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/3088/images/supine%20suspended.jpg",
                  "caption": ""
                }
              ],
              "importance": "4",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "prone",
              "table": null,
              "images": [
                {
                  "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/3088/images/prone.jpg",
                  "caption": ""
                }
              ],
              "importance": "4",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "lateral decubitus",
              "table": null,
              "images": [
                {
                  "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/3088/images/lat%20decubitus.jpg",
                  "caption": ""
                }
              ],
              "importance": "4",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Anesthesia",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "**general anesthesia** (allows muscle relaxation and placement of patient in prone or lateral decubitus position)",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "4",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "regional anesthesia may be used; it does not allow for immediate evaluation of nerve function after surgery and patients may not tolerate the uncomfortable position for a prolonged period",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Technique and Portals",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Portal placement technique",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "**fully distend joint** through lateral soft spot before placing portals",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "4",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "capsule distension moves NV structures away from the joint when trocar is introduced",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "1",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "**careful \"nick and spread\"** technique using hemostat",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "4",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "**posterior medial portal** usually avoided due to proximity to ulnar nerve",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "5",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Elbow position",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "establish anterior portals with elbow flexed 90deg",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "establish posterior portals in some extension",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Standard 30deg arthroscope",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "text": "Tourniquet",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "text": "Solid cannulas are helpful to maintain fluid distension and avoid fluid extravasation into soft tissue (versus trephinated)",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "text": "Landmarks: olecranon, lateral and medial epicondyles, radiocapitellar joint, ulnar nerve",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "mark out before insufflating joint as distension can alter position",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Summary of portals",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "portal selection depends on the underlying pathology",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "after joint insufflation, establish either medially- or laterally-based viewing portal, then establish working portal under direct visualization via needle localization.",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "establishing a medially-based portal first, prior to joint/soft-tissue swelling, may be advantageous to avoid neurovascular injury",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "",
          "table": [
            {
